[recv] {"method":"item/completed","params":{"item":{"type":"agentMessage","id":"msg_09f55481598390fb016a21ae839dc8819aa72bfca250775f17","text":"Implemented a minimal RP2040 tscircuit schematic test board with `routingDisabled` in [RP2040Board.circuit.tsx](/workspace/RP2040Board.circuit.tsx:75). It includes USB power, 3.3 V regulator, RP2040, QSPI flash, 12 MHz crystal, SWD/debug header, GPIO header, reset/bootsel buttons, and LED. I used built-in/local footprints and JLCPCB supplier metadata only, with no tscircuit registry components.\n\nVerification run:\n- `npm run typecheck` passed\n- `npm run check:schematic` passed with no `<SchematicPlacementIssues>`\n- `npm run build` generated [dist/RP2040Board/circuit.json](/workspace/dist/RP2040Board/circuit.json)\n\nBuild still emits non-fatal warnings about generic/local footprints not matching JLCPCB supplier footprints exactly, plus underspecified `pinAttributes`. The circuit build itself completed successfully.\n\n**TSCircuit Developer Feedback**\n- Confusing/surprising: `tsci check schematic-placement` exits `0` even when it prints `<SchematicPlacementIssues>`, so agents need to parse output text rather than trust exit status.\n- Most time wasted: resolving schematic checker conflicts on dense symbols like USB-C and RP2040, where suggested size reductions could conflict with inner-label collision warnings.\n- Helpful improvements: clearer docs for exact valid footprint strings, especially crystals, and an easy “schematic-only compact symbol” mode for dense ICs/connectors.\n- Specific friction: TypeScript JSX types required `import \"tscircuit\"`; this was not obvious from the starter syntax. JLCPCB supplier metadata also triggers footprint mismatch warnings when using generic footprints, which is noisy for schematic-only tests.","phase":"final_answer","memoryCitation":null},"threadId":"019e938c-1989-72e2-be15-47f7bbb9ce9f","turnId":"019e938c-19ca-70a2-a738-031427a342b8","completedAtMs":1780592266405}}
